# [SPACE](../wg/space.html)

## Summary

The SPACE Research Group (SPACE RG) held an interim meeting hosted by ESA at the European Space Operations Center (ESOC) in Darmstadt, Germany. The meeting brought together researchers, space agency operational leads, and industrial standardization experts to discuss the convergence of Internet protocol architectures with space networks and non-terrestrial networks (NTN). 

Key topics included:
- Transport protocol integration (QUIC convergence layers for Delay-Tolerant Networking / Bundle Protocol).
- Environmental sustainability and carbon footprint accounting of LEO satellite constellations and in-orbit edge compute.
- Real-world simulation and routing validation of mega-constellations using discrete-event frameworks (OMNeT++/INET).
- Operational lessons from interplanetary communication networks (the ESA/NASA Mars Relay Network).
- Security architectures and trust boundaries in 3GPP 5G/6G NTN and Open RAN (O-RAN).
- Scalable routing logic in time-varying networks, comparing link-state, geographic checkpoint routing, and segment routing.
- Architectural principles for onboard IP routers ("flying subnets") and IETF standard technology adoption in constellation networks.

