### Session 1: Research Talks

#### Earth-to-Moon Networking: Comparison of End-to-End and DTN Approaches
* **Presenter**: Tomaso de Cola (DLR)
* **Slide Deck**: [Earth-to-Moon Networking: Comparison of End-to-End and DTN Approaches (Tomaso de Cola, DLR)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-earth-to-moon-networking-comparison-of-end-to-end-and-dtn-approaches-tomaso-de-cola-dlr-00)

Tomaso de Cola presented research conducted with the University of Bologna on running the Bundle Protocol (BPv6 [RFC 5050] / BPv7 [RFC 9171]) over a custom QUIC Convergence Layer (QUIC-CL). 
* **Protocol Design**: QUIC-CL builds on QUIC features (RFC 9000, RFC 9001) such as native TLS encryption, multiplexed streams, and datagram extensions. It defines three service levels:
  1. *Unreliable Service*: Maps bundles directly to QUIC datagrams.
  2. *Reliable Service*: Relies on native QUIC stream reliability and congestion control.
  3. *Notified Service*: Uses stream-level signaling to provide delivery notifications, allowing upper-layer BP mechanisms (e.g., custodial transfer, status reporting) to trigger retransmissions.
* **QoS Mapping**: Mapped extended Class of Service (ECOS) priorities from BP to distinct QUIC streams.
* **Validation**: Initial prototype validation was completed using PicoQUIC across simulated LEO, LEO-GEO, and Earth-Moon topology scenarios.
* **Discussion**:
  * Michael Welzl asked about the standardization status of ECOS. Tomaso de Cola clarified that ECOS was standardized in the CCSDS BPv6 annex and is being updated for BPv7 framework specifications.
  * Joerg Ott raised concerns regarding MTU constraints when mapping large bundles into QUIC datagrams for unreliable transfer. Tomaso acknowledged that datagram size limitations require careful payload sizing or leveraging unreliable stream resets, which will be evaluated in follow-up work.

#### Space for Sustainability?
* **Presenter**: Andreas Schmidt (Saarland University)
* **Slide Deck**: [Space for Sustainability? (Andreas Schmidt, Saarland University)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-space-for-sustainability-andreas-schmidt-saarland-university-00)

Andreas Schmidt analyzed the lifecycle environmental impact of LEO satellite constellations and proposed orbital data centers across four phases: production, launch, operation, and reentry.
* **Emissions Accounting**:
  * Launch intensity for a Falcon 9 payload contributes ~35.5 kg CO₂eq per kg of payload, while reentry atmospheric burnup contributes ~16 kg CO₂eq per kg (primarily due to $NO_x$ production and alumina particle degradation).
  * Solar power in LEO carries an embodied carbon intensity of ~66 g CO₂eq/kWh (accounting for panel/battery launch mass and degradation), compared to ~34 g CO₂eq/kWh for terrestrial solar.
* **Thermal Dissipation Bottleneck**: Radiative heat transfer in a vacuum requires substantial physical radiator area/mass. A 1 kW GPU node (e.g., NVIDIA H100) weighing 3 kg requires ~30 kg of radiator equipment, making thermal control account for 56% of the compute node's total carbon footprint.
* **Hardware Lifecycle**: High compute hardware failure/degradation rates (3-year GPU lifespan vs. 5-year satellite platform lifespan) force inefficient redundancy trade-offs. Schmidt presented the open-source `ESpace` simulator modeling these parameters.
* **Discussion**:
  * Felix Flentge asked if transceiver power and communication volume were included. Schmidt confirmed transceiver mass is modeled, though communication traffic volume requires further scenario modeling.
  * Nishanth Sastry asked about future hardware paradigms such as superconductive quantum computing in space.
  * Joerg Ott and Juan Fraire discussed orbit capacity constraints in Sun-Synchronous Orbits (SSO) and the distinction between Total Ionizing Dose (TID) and Single Event Latch-ups (SEL) on unshielded commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) components.

#### Simulating Satellite Constellations and Aircraft with Real-World Data
* **Presenter**: Marcin Bosk (TUM)
* **Slide Deck**: [Simulating Satellite Constellations and Aircraft with Real-World Data (Marcin Bosk, TUM)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-simulating-satellite-constellations-and-aircraft-with-real-world-data-marcin-bosk-tum-00)

Marcin Bosk presented an OMNeT++/INET discrete-event simulation framework capable of processing real-world TLE datasets and ground tracks to model high-density satellite and aeronautical networks.
* **Framework Architecture**: 
  * Ingests full TLE sets (~10,000 Starlink satellites) and uses the LEONET algorithm to recover Walker Delta constellation grids, inter-satellite links (ISLs, using 4-link plus-grid topologies), and shell dynamics.
  * Treats satellites and ground stations as standard IPv4/IPv6 routers inheriting native INET networking stacks.
  * Implements a global control-plane Oracle computing delay-optimal routes deployed out-of-band.
* **Validation**: Simulated a path between Reunion Island and Frankfurt. The simulation achieved end-to-end RTTs between 100–200 ms, closely matching empirical measurements published by Mohammad Rajiullah et al. (2023). Applied the model to a transatlantic flight path (NYC to Frankfurt) connected via LEO GSLs.
* **Discussion**:
  * Nishanth Sastry noted that matching empirical latency data without modeled processing delays suggests current commercial ISL utilization may be low.
  * Matthias asked whether link breakage logic was evaluated; Bosk confirmed the global Oracle accounts for link state transitions (e.g., polar orbit crossings).
  * Roland Bliss commented on source routing versus per-hop routing convergence, pointing out Starlink's 15-second routing update intervals.

#### Mars Relay Network: An Operational Example
* **Presenter**: Silvia Sangiorgi (ESA)
* **Slide Deck**: [Mars Relay Network: An Operational Example (Silvia Sangiorgi, ESA)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-mars-relay-network-an-operational-example-silvia-sangiorgi-esa-00)

Silvia Sangiorgi shared operational insights from the joint ESA/NASA Mars Relay Network, which handles over 99% of science data returned from the Martian surface via orbiting relays (such as ESA’s Trace Gas Orbiter [TGO] and Mars Express).
* **Protocol & Operational Mechanics**:
  * Uses CCSDS Proximity-1 over the UHF proximity link. Key features include adaptive data rates based on signal strength, hailing mechanisms for link setup, and selection between reliable (ARQ) and unreliable transmission modes.
  * Relies on a centralized 5-stage coordination process: Strategic Planning (months out), Tactical Planning (2-week window), Forward Link execution, Telemetry Return, and Performance Analysis.
  * Operational tools include MAROS (NASA's central hub) and ERCO (ESA's internal coordination node).
* **Future Requirements**: Upcoming surface assets (e.g., the 2028 Rosalind Franklin rover and NASA Mars helicopters) will lack Direct-to-Earth (DTE) communication capabilities, requiring proximity-link time correlation and automated relay scheduling.
* **Discussion**:
  * Juan Fraire asked if un-planned proximity link establishment is supported. Sangiorgi explained that link creation currently requires pre-planned orbiter attitude pointing, though surface assets maintain standby flexibility to respond to incoming hails.

