### Session 2: Industrial and European Directions

#### Securing Connected Space: 3GPP NTN from 5G to 6G
* **Presenters**: Emile Stephan, Clifton Fernandes (Nokia)
* **Slide Deck**: [Securing connected space: 3GPP NTN from 5G to 6G (Emrah Tomur and Clifton Fernandes, Nokia)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-securing-connected-space-3gpp-ntn-from-5g-to-6g-emrah-tomur-and-clifton-fernandes-nokia-01)

Emile Stephan and Clifton Fernandes outlined the evolution of Non-Terrestrial Networks within 3GPP standards, moving from 5G additions (Rel-17/18) to native 6G integration (Rel-21).
* **Architecture & Security Boundaries**:
  * Differentiated between *Transparent Payload* (bent-pipe RF repeaters) and *Regenerative Payload* (onboard gNodeB base stations or core network functions).
  * 3GPP SA3 has initiated a dedicated Security Study Area (Security Area 11, working under TR 23.801) to evaluate NTN security.
  * Key security challenges: moving cell patterns crossing national PLMN borders, authentication timers over long/variable propagation delays, tracking area updates without continuous GNSS, and disaggregated Open RAN (O-RAN) split interfaces (O-RU, O-DU, O-CU) across orbital and ground entities.
* **Discussion**:
  * Joerg Ott asked about specific trust boundary challenges when network functions are hosted in orbit. Clifton Fernandes highlighted the multi-tenant trust boundaries between Satellite Network Operators (SNOs) and Mobile Network Operators (MNOs).
  * Ahmad Mahmod and Matthias discussed regulatory spectrum licensing and cross-border beam projection constraints.

#### Routing in Time-Varying Networks
* **Presenter**: Paulo Mendes (Airbus)
* **Slide Deck**: [Routing in Time-Varying Networks (Paulo Mendes, Airbus)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-routing-in-time-varying-networks-paulo-mendes-airbus-00)

Paulo Mendes examined routing approaches for dynamic, time-varying flying networks (LEO constellations, aeronautical meshes).
* **Current Operational Reality**: Commercial constellations (e.g., Starlink) largely utilize "hot-potato" ground landing, routing traffic immediately to the nearest terrestrial gateway/POP rather than performing multi-hop in-space network routing.
* **Routing Scalability**: Standard link-state protocols (OSPF, IS-IS) struggle to scale across dynamic global satellite topologies. Topological clustering algorithms (e.g., spectral clustering) exhibit high computational overhead.
* **Proposed Architecture**: Introduced *Geographic Checkpoint Routing* integrated with Segment Routing (SRv6 / MPLS). Ground/aircraft service edges compute routes based on geographic checkpoints rather than individual IP topological hops, allowing satellites to execute source routing along spatial zones.
* **Discussion**:
  * Michael Welzl inquired about packet encapsulation roles. Mendes clarified that encapsulation occurs at service edges (terminals or aircraft gateways), keeping satellite payload processing minimal.
  * *Chat context*: Felix Flentge and Juan Fraire noted that while the IETF TVR (Time-Varying Routing) WG defines data models for time-evolving topologies, it does not define specific routing algorithms.

#### Towards Constellation Networks Built from IETF Technologies
* **Presenter**: Théo Tchilinguirian (Aerospace Lab)
* **Slide Deck**: [Towards Constellation Networks built from IETF Technologies](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-towards-constellation-networks-built-from-ietf-technologies-00)

Théo Tchilinguirian presented Aerospace Lab's perspective on building constellation networks using standardized IETF protocol stacks.
* **Onboard Architecture**: Modeled modern satellites as "flying subnets" centered around a radiation-hardened central IP router connecting internal payload units (gNodeB splits, management hosts) and external interfaces (ISLs, feeder links).
* **Key Principles for IP Convergence**:
  1. *Native IP Forwarding*: Space networks should expose standard IP hop-by-hop forwarding services.
  2. *Differentiated Treatment & Transparency*: Maintain standard ICMP signaling, ECN marking, Path MTU Discovery, QoS mapping, and network slicing (IETF network slices).
  3. *Reuse of Terrestrial Tooling*: Adopt existing IETF Network Management and OAM protocols.
* **Ongoing Contributions**: Introduced a CDDL specification draft (`draft-tchilinguirian-space-constellation-cddl`) for standardized constellation topology descriptions. Previewed a CoNEXT paper detailing link-state micro-loop prevention using ordered FIB updates and multicast tree topology distribution.
* **Discussion**:
  * Joerg Ott pointed out that carrying IP routing state across space segments conflicts with local terrestrial POP breakouts due to IP address prefix aggregation limits.

#### Introducing the NexaSphere Project
* **Presenter**: Tomaso de Cola (DLR)
* **Slide Deck**: [Introducing the NexaSphere project (Tomaso de Cola, DLR)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-space-01/materials/slides-interim-2026-space-01-sessa-introducing-the-nexasphere-project-tomaso-de-cola-dlr-00)

Tomaso de Cola introduced *NexaSphere*, a 36-month EU Horizon SNS JU project (16 partners) focused on harmonizing 3D NTN/TN networks within the 6G architecture.
* **Focus Areas**: Multi-connectivity across aviation, automotive, and railway verticals; free-space optical (FSO) feeder/inter-satellite links; onboard train/plane LiFi integration; disaggregated RAN splits; and AI-driven cross-domain network orchestration.
* **Demonstration Roadmap**: Subsystem demonstrations planned for IEEE/EUCNC 2026 (June in Dublin), leading to final proof-of-concept validation in late 2026.

